WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.820
Cancellation and timeouts are resource-management controls.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:14.840
They define how long work may consume CPU, memory, locks, connections, external service capacity, and user attention.

3
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:23.900
Without these controls, work can continue after the caller is gone, after shutdown has started, or after a dependency has stopped responding in a useful time.

4
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.940
Cancellation in .NET is cooperative.

5
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:35.960
A token communicates that the caller no longer wants the work to continue, but the called code must observe the token and stop at safe points.

6
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:47.020
Passing cancellation through supported call chains helps requests, background jobs, database operations, HTTP calls, streams, and queues release resources sooner.

7
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.780
Timeouts prevent indefinite waits.

8
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:58.600
They should be applied to external calls, lock acquisition, queue operations, background workflows, and any operation where waiting forever would be harmful.

9
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:05.340
Timeout behavior should be tested with cleanup and error handling so partial work does not leave inconsistent state.

10
00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:10.100
Bounded concurrency protects dependencies and the application itself.

11
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:22.440
Worker counts, request limits, connection pool limits, queue capacity, semaphore limits, and rate limits all help prevent one feature from consuming capacity needed by the rest of the system.

12
00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:27.660
Limits should be paired with clear failure behavior so callers receive predictable outcomes.

13
00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.900
Work queues need ownership and recovery rules.

14
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:40.360
A queue should define who can enqueue work, how much it can hold, how failures are recorded, when retries stop, and what happens to messages that cannot be processed.

15
00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:46.700
Poison-message handling and dead-letter behavior may be appropriate for workflows where retrying forever would hide the real problem.

16
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:53.460
Cleanup should preserve consistent application state after cancellation, timeout, or partial failure.

17
00:01:53.560 --> 00:02:04.920
That may mean releasing locks, rolling back transactions, deleting temporary files, returning buffers, closing streams, marking work as incomplete, or scheduling safe follow-up.

18
00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:08.940
Resource limits are most effective when recovery paths are designed with them.
